  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "B.Mahesh Reddy vs State Of Ap on 16 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.5294 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "1. The petitioners/accused Nos.3 and 4 seek to quash the proceedings against them in Crime No.266 of 2019 of Kothapet Police Station, Guntur Urban, which was registered against them and others for the offences under Sections 188, 272, 273, 328 and 420 R/w.34 of IPC and Sections 58, 59 and 63 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (for short, 'the FSS Act'). 2. The allegations against the petitioners briefly are that, on 29.072019 at about 06.00 P.M., on receiving the credible information, the complainant suo motu registered the case against accused Nos.1 to 4 on the ground that A-1 and A-2 are in possession of the banned tobacco products, belonging to A-3 and A-4, illegally transporting the same in the TATA Ace vehicle bearing No.AP07TG0114 and on interrogation as they voluntarily confessed to have been engaged in the business of prohibited tobacco products, seized the same, registered F.I.R., and remanded A-1 and A-2 to judicial custody for the offences stated supra. 3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore the police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act.",
    "arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate."
  },
